// Foliage. A real material rather than a shade of `wood`, because its
// *physics* genuinely differ on four numbers that already exist -- which is
// this engine's own test for when a material is warranted
// (`material.rs`: behaviour comes from `kind` plus numeric parameters).
// `Reports/plant-substrate-v2-design.md` §6a.
//
// Produced by `Grow`'s plastochron, laterally off a shoot, never as part of
// the stem itself. `CellType::Leaf` is what carries `Photosynthesize`; this
// is what that cell is made of.
(
    name: "leaf",
    display: "Leaf",
    kind: Plant,
    // Climbable, like the wood it hangs off. Included deliberately rather
    // than left solid: a pure ladder needs no "is this branch thick enough
    // to stand on" rule, and that rule would be shape-inference of exactly
    // the kind four support models already failed at. It is also what makes
    // a crown catch a fall (`Tuning::canopy_drag`) instead of stopping one.
    climbable: true,
    // A crown catches you. Per tick at full immersion, so a clip through
    // the top of one barely registers and a drop through the middle of one
    // arrests him -- see `MaterialDef::fall_drag`. Wood deliberately has
    // none: branches slow a fall, a bare trunk does not.
    fall_drag: 0.15,
    // Foliage is not wood. Well under `wood`'s 0.9.
    density: 0.25,
    // **Eight hue bands of four tonal steps** — see
    // `organism::PALETTE_BAND`.
    // A species declares which bands it draws from (`foliage_bands`) and an
    // individual draws one band inside that range at germination, so a
    // stand is a spread of related greens instead of sixteen draws of one.
    //
    // Band 2 is the original four-colour palette, unchanged, so anything
    // that referenced a specific shade index by hand still lands on the
    // colour it was written against — and a species with no declared range
    // still draws uniformly over the whole thing, as before.
    //
    // The order is deliberate: hue runs blue-green → green → yellow-green →
    // olive, so a species' range is a contiguous slice of one axis and
    // widening a range never jumps a hue.
    //
    // **Bands 6-7 step off that hue axis rather than continuing it**, and
    // the break is deliberate. Six bands is two short of what four woody
    // species need to hold *disjoint* two-band ranges, and two of them —
    // `shrub` and `creeper` — declared the identical `(first: 4, count: 2)`
    // until 2026-08-23, so a bush and a ground-runner were literally the
    // same colour (`Reports/plant-project-review-2026-08-23.md` §2.1: it is
    // colour and composition, not the architectural levers, that decide
    // whether a stand reads as more than one plant). Continuing past dark
    // olive means going brown, which reads as dead foliage; going
    // *desaturated* instead gives a sage that is unmistakably a leaf and
    // unmistakably not one of the four saturated greens. The contiguous-
    // slice property still holds where it is used — inside each species'
    // own range — which is all `bark_band_for_density` and the leaf-economy
    // locus ever read.
    colors: [
        // 0: deep blue-green — fir, spruce
        (42, 74, 52),
        (52, 90, 62),
        (34, 62, 44),
        (60, 102, 72),
        // 1: mid blue-green — pine
        (46, 86, 58),
        (56, 102, 70),
        (38, 72, 48),
        (66, 116, 80),
        // 2: mid green — the original palette
        (58, 96, 40),
        (70, 116, 48),
        (48, 82, 34),
        (82, 132, 56),
        // 3: yellow-green — birch, willow
        (86, 118, 44),
        (102, 138, 54),
        (72, 100, 36),
        (118, 158, 66),
        // 4: olive — dry-site shrubs
        (94, 104, 56),
        (110, 122, 68),
        (78, 88, 46),
        (126, 140, 80),
        // 5: dark olive
        (66, 76, 38),
        (80, 92, 46),
        (54, 62, 30),
        (94, 108, 56),
        // 6: pale sage — glaucous, ivy and the ground layer
        (104, 126, 100),
        (120, 144, 116),
        (86, 106, 82),
        (138, 162, 134),
        // 7: deep sage
        (76, 98, 76),
        (90, 114, 90),
        (62, 82, 62),
        (106, 132, 106),
    ],
    // Much more flammable than heartwood, and it burns *out* far faster. A
    // real canopy fire runs through foliage, not through trunks, and this
    // is what lets the engine's existing Drossel-Schwabl forest-fire loop
    // express that: leaves carry the fire between crowns while the wood
    // beneath them survives a fast-moving front.
    flammability: 0.75,
    burn_temperature: 550.0,
    burn_duration: 40,
    burns_into: "ash",
    heat_conductivity: 0.2,
    // **One, and it is deliberately not `wood`'s 8.** `structural.rs`
    // treats every `Plant`-kind cell as body material, so a leaf does take
    // part in the support search; the design doc's §6a is explicit that a
    // leaf must not be a load path, since "treating it as a load path
    // would let a canopy hold up a trunk". A span of 1 means a leaf holds
    // up nothing but itself and drops the moment its own attachment goes,
    // which is the closest this model gets to "not structural" without a
    // separate exemption in the traversal.
    // Inert-path capacity, unchanged. A stray leaf cell that has lost its
    // organism holds up nothing.
    max_unsupported_span: 1,
    // **No `max_cantilever_reach`, which means opted out of the organism
    // cantilever rule entirely** -- the default is `u16::MAX`.
    //
    // This field's ancestor was the `max_unsupported_span: 1` above, and it
    // read correctly against the old support search, where the number was
    // hops from the cell to the nearest ground contact and no code path
    // ever scheduled a structural check on a leaf.
    //
    // `plant::anchor_support` changed what the number is measured against:
    // it is now the cell's reach along its own load path from the plant's
    // anchors, which for any leaf in a crown is tens of cells. The first
    // run
    // with checks actually firing took median leaves per tree from 1,376 to
    // **1**, and the stand from 31,731 cells to 7,171 once the income went
    // with them.
    //
    // Opting out does not exempt a leaf from *attachment*:
    // `structural::organism_structural_tick` tests that first and
    // separately, so cutting the twig a spray hangs from still brings the
    // spray down. That is the property the note above actually wanted -- a
    // leaf is not a load path -- now enforced rather than approximated by a
    // small number.
    // **Litter, not deadwood, and no `woody` above.** A crown is roughly a
    // third foliage by cell count (10,136 of 28,421 on the measured stand),
    // and a leaf is not a small log: it has different physics on four
    // numbers already and it must not sit on the fragment ladder padding a
    // draw into a piece that is mostly foliage. So severed foliage goes
    // down `breaks_into` as scatter while the wood beside it goes on the
    // ladder -- the third of the three tiers in
    // `Reports/physical-trees-design-2026-08-23.md` §5.3.
    //
    // `deadwood` was the target before this and was simply the only debris
    // material that existed when leaves got one. `litter` is what the decay
    // and ant layers already read (`litter.ron`'s own closing note records
    // that nothing wrote it yet), so this is the switch-over that note is
    // waiting for as much as it is a felling change.
    breaks_into: "litter",
    // **A leaf that rides down on its branch stays a leaf when it lands.**
    // `severs_into` is consulted only for a cell that left the grid as
    // organism tissue, so this is the landing half of foliage riding with
    // the piece it hangs on -- see `rigid::fell_severed_tissue` for why it
    // rides at all.
    //
    // **Deliberately *not* the same target as `breaks_into` any more**, and
    // that split is the point. Owner ruling, watching a fell (card
    // `20260829T034016675Z-5a9b07`): *"The leaves shouldn't turn to powder
    // ever. They can stay on the branch if that is easier (but if they do
    // fall off, i feel like they should stay as larger leaf pieces)."*
    // Sending both paths to `litter` meant a limb came down whole and then
    // dissolved into grain the instant it stopped -- measured at `litter`
    // 992 cells against `log` 868 on the settled pile, i.e. the foliage
    // outweighing the wood it arrived attached to.
    //
    // So the ride now ends in `deadleaf`, the foliage piece tier, which
    // lies where it fell and reaches `litter` by *decaying* instead of by
    // being pulverised. `breaks_into` below is unchanged and still goes
    // straight to `litter`: that path is a leaf no piece ever reached,
    // scattering in mid-air, and a `Solid` created in mid-air would hang
    // there.
    severs_into: "deadleaf",

    // **Foliage bends, and it is the only part of a tree that does.**
    //
    // Set against the foliage's own measured moments rather than the
    // stand's
    // pooled ones, which is a distinction that cost a wrong reading: trunks
    // dominate a pooled distribution and foliage sits two orders of
    // magnitude
    // below it. Censused on `scene=grove` (`filmstrip`'s per-material
    // line),
    // 5,265 and 10,189 leaf cells on two frames:
    //
    //     p50 4.1-5.9   p90 260-348   p99 2,393-4,923   max 12,614-30,494
    //
    // 1000 sits between p90 and p99, so the few percent of foliage that is
    // genuinely carrying a loaded cluster on a long lever lies over in a
    // gust, and the quiet interior of a crown does not creep. Set in the
    // gap
    // rather than on a measured value, per `CLAUDE.md`.
    //
    // **Why foliage and not wood**, when a limb sagging before it breaks is
    // the more wanted picture: a bend relieves its moment by shortening a
    // *horizontal* lever, and a trunk's wind moment is a vertical one that
    // a
    // one-cell lean does not shorten. So a trunk given a stiffness would
    // lean
    // again every tick for as long as the wind blew, with nothing in the
    // model to stop it. What stops a real trunk is that it breaks, and that
    // is stage 3's business. A leaf cluster has no such problem: it hangs
    // off
    // the end of everything, its moment is nearly all wind, and swinging it
    // downwind takes that load off directly.
    stiffness: 1000.0,

    // Foliage: what a herbivore is for. 120 is what `ant.ron`'s
    // `eat_energy` paid for any mouthful, so the ant's economy is unchanged
    // by the move from eater-constant to food-property.
    food_energy: 480.0,
    // **The armour table's soft band -- see `material.rs`'s
    // `penetration_resistance` doc for the whole derivation.** Everything
    // a shipped mouth must be able to eat is priced below the *weakest*
    // shipped bite (`beetle.ron`'s `dig_force: 0.3`), because `act`'s
    // ingest gate is one branch for all food: a food priced above every
    // bite force in the world is not armoured, it is inedible.
    //
    // In MPa like every other value of this field. Live soft tissue: a
    // lamina punctures at almost nothing.
    penetration_resistance: 0.1,
    food_class: -1.0,
)
